  repos/npm is an on-demand cache rather than a bulk index, so it grows when a
  run asks for a name it lacks; eval/npm/seed.sh deliberately fills a symlink
  farm under the run directory instead, leaving this tree untouched, and the
  measured sweeps then run against a frozen shim.  A changed hash here means
  something wrote into the checkout.

cargo body cache (eval/cargo/valid.sh):

  The cargo validity check runs its repair `--offline`, so the crate bodies in
  CARGO_HOME are part of the universe it answers about, the way the index
  checkout is.  eval/cargo/warm.sh fills them in one online pass; record what
  that pass left behind:

  21535cd8a1f84cf60c3641de81c4ba716361dd116d528f37f22ca47c983a50cf   820 crates, 155M

  cd "$CARGO_CMP_OUT/cargo-home/registry/cache" \
    && find . -name '*.crate' -printf '%f\n' | LC_ALL=C sort | sha256sum

  Recorded from the warm pass over all 28 goals of eval/cargo/goals.txt, the
  one the 28/28 sweep then ran offline against.  It covers more than the goals'
  own answers: warming runs the same repair cargo runs when scoring, so the
  cache holds the bodies of the repaired resolve -- the unactivated optionals a
  Cargo.lock carries included.  The hash previously left blank here was never
  filled because the check it belonged to asked cargo to accept our lock
  unchanged and `cargo fetch --locked` downloads nothing for a lock it rejects,
  so a warm pass covered only the 5 goals that passed.

  Filenames, not contents, and basenames, not paths.  Each body is checksummed
  against the index on download and repos/crates.io-index is already pinned by
  commit above, so the bytes are pinned transitively; what that does not pin is
  *which* crates are present, and with the scoring pass offline that is exactly
  what decides whether a goal can be answered at all.  Basenames also keep the
  hash independent of the registry directory cargo derives from the proxy's URL
  and port, which is local to a run rather than to the snapshot.

  A body missing from the cache is reported NOCACHE rather than scored, so a
  short cache shows up as an unanswered goal and never as an invalid one.
